Workaround: Manual OAuth token exchange script

  Ran into the same issue on Linux (WSL2, OpenCode 1.2.27). Installing opencode-anthropic-oauth@0.3.0 and adding it to
  config wasn't enough ΓÇö the built-in opencode-anthropic-auth@0.0.13 loads first and claims the Anthropic provider
  route, so the community plugin's token exchange never runs. The auth flow still hits Anthropic with the wrong
  User-Agent: anthropic header ΓåÆ instant 429.

  What fixed it: Bypassing OpenCode's plugin/auth system entirely with a standalone Node.js script that does the PKCE
  OAuth flow manually using the correct User-Agent: claude-cli/2.1.80 (external, cli) header, then writes the tokens
  directly to ~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json.

  Script: https://gist.github.com/ (see below)

  #!/usr/bin/env node
  import { randomBytes, createHash } from "node:crypto";
  import { writeFileSync, mkdirSync } from "node:fs";
  import { createInterface } from "node:readline";
  import { homedir } from "node:os";
  import { join } from "node:path";

  const CLIENT_ID = "9d1c250a-e61b-44d9-88ed-5944d1962f5e";
  const TOKEN_URL = "https://console.anthropic.com/v1/oauth/token";
  const REDIRECT_URI = "https://console.anthropic.com/oauth/code/callback";
  const USER_AGENT = "claude-cli/2.1.80 (external, cli)";

  const base64url = (buf) => buf.toString("base64url").replace(/=+$/, "");
  const verifier = base64url(randomBytes(32));
  const challenge = base64url(createHash("sha256").update(verifier).digest());

  const params = new URLSearchParams({
    code: "true", response_type: "code", client_id: CLIENT_ID,
    redirect_uri: REDIRECT_URI,
    scope: "org:create_api_key user:profile user:inference",
    code_challenge: challenge, code_challenge_method: "S256", state: verifier,
  });

  console.log("\nOpen this URL:\n");
  console.log(`https://claude.ai/oauth/authorize?${params}`);
  console.log("\nPaste the authorization code:\n");

  const rl = createInterface({ input: process.stdin, output: process.stdout });
  rl.question("Code: ", async (raw) => {
    rl.close();
    const code = raw.includes("#") ? raw.slice(0, raw.indexOf("#")).trim() : raw.trim();
    const body = new URLSearchParams({
      grant_type: "authorization_code", code, code_verifier: verifier,
      client_id: CLIENT_ID, redirect_uri: REDIRECT_URI, state: verifier,
    });
    const res = await fetch(TOKEN_URL, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/x-www-form-urlencoded", "User-Agent": USER_AGENT },
      body: body.toString(),
    });
    if (!res.ok) { console.error(await res.text()); process.exit(1); }
    const data = await res.json();
    const authDir = join(homedir(), ".local", "share", "opencode");
    mkdirSync(authDir, { recursive: true });
    writeFileSync(join(authDir, "auth.json"), JSON.stringify({
      anthropic: { type: "oauth", refresh: data.refresh_token, access: data.access_token, expires: Date.now() +
  data.expires_in * 1000 }
    }, null, 2));
    console.log("\nDone! Tokens saved. Start OpenCode normally.");
  });

  Usage: node fix-anthropic-auth.mjs ΓåÆ open URL ΓåÆ authorize ΓåÆ paste code ΓåÆ tokens saved.

  Why the plugin alone doesn't work: Both the built-in and community plugins register provider: "anthropic". The
  built-in loads first (it's bundled), so opencode auth login routes through the old code with the blocked User-Agent.
  The community plugin only gets to handle the loader hook (token refresh, API calls) ΓÇö not the initial auth. So you
  need to bootstrap the initial tokens yourself, and then the community plugin handles refreshes fine after that.
